The frozen code matches commit `ee39c2a`; regenerating the summary reproduces it exactly. All 16 tests pass. I independently recomputed all 172 E4 uncovered sets, sampled E1 configurations, checked the exponent arithmetic, and reproduced the matched-control and held-out analyses. Verification: [script](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/inversesieve_2/verify_referee.py), [output](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/inversesieve_2/verification.out).

| Claim | Verdict | Required fix |
|---|---|---|
| §0: general “kill”; structural impossibility of this route | **WRONG** | The results establish restricted locality barriers and negative evidence for selected statistics. They do not exclude arbitrary N-dependent container generators or a new inverse theorem. Replace the headline with “no constructive compression found; tested families show little useful enrichment.” |
| Exponent ledger | **CORRECT** | Exact arithmetic confirms \(6/31,\ 7/124,\ 69/124,\ 55/62,\ 4/31,\ k\ge7/2,\ 3/124\). Their interpretations require the qualifications below. |
| S(a) | **FIX** | Explicitly assume the **full signed primitive height box**, with \(1\le |A|\le H,\ 1\le B\le H\). Sign symmetry alone gives neither the displayed union nor inversion symmetry. Counterexample: \(H=2,\ell=11,V=\{(\pm1,1),(\pm2,1)\}\); \(F=\{1,2,9,10\}\), but \(F^{-1}=\{1,5,6,10\}\). The displayed formula also accidentally permits \(A=0\). |
| S(b) | **CORRECT** | Given nonzero slopes, injectivity, sign symmetry, \(\ell\equiv3\bmod4\), and \(\ell\nmid N\), the cardinality identity follows. Call the product an independent-model density; it is not the exact density within the finite interval. |
| S(c) | **FIX** | Correct for the full primitive box and \(\ell\nmid pq\). For an arbitrary sign-symmetric subset V, existence of a short lattice vector need not mean that its weight belongs to V. |
| S(d) | **CORRECT** | The eight-lift argument works. For completeness, explain that products of two elements of \(F'\) are quadratic residues, so equality to the negative of another such product is impossible. |
| S(e) | **CORRECT** | The measured energies match the raw data and summary. Keep \(H^4\log H\) explicitly heuristic; rational lifting under \(4H^4<\ell\) does not itself prove that asymptotic. |
| S(f) | **FIX** | Multiplicative-energy transport is proved. Additive pseudorandomness is supported only by the measured statistic and tested designs; square-rooting has not been proved to erase all useful additive structure. |
| Prop C | **CORRECT** | The contraposition is exact. It does not establish equivalent computational difficulty. An N-dependent candidate set’s small density within I does not imply a small exceptional fraction of semiprime inputs. Require efficient construction and enumeration separately. |
| Prop E | **CORRECT** | Correct within the stated independent model. Freeze a common design/intensity over the dyadic range, or retain sums of \(e^{-\Lambda_N}\). Arithmetic completeness remains heuristic. |
| Prop M | **FIX** | The exponent comparison and fourth-moment bookkeeping are correct. “Not explicit” should mean “not efficiently enumerable within the proposed budget”: U already has an explicit membership test. The stated mass budget yields \(N^{55/124+o(1)}\), without the extra \(-\varepsilon\), unless shorter containers are assumed. |
| Theorem L: counting argument | **FIX** | Valid with fixed dyadic \(P=2^{n/2}\), aligned cells, and the stated mass convention. For arbitrary x, coprimality is false; restrict to prime candidates or units first. This restriction preserves pair coverage and decreases mass. State these steps explicitly. |
| L: “WLOG” dyadic refinement and \(\Delta\ge2M\) | **WRONG** | These are restrictions, not a valid general reduction. Dyadic partitions need not refine arbitrary cells; increasing a too-small \(\Delta\) loses information. Boundary cells genuinely break the bound: \(n=20,P=1024,M=1,\Delta=524280\), with the sole output \(\{911\}\) in cell \(k=2\), catches \((911,1151)\). Its n-bit mass is 16, whereas the claimed bound requires 128. |
| L: sub-rule accounting | **FIX** | The summation proof needs no additional factor for the number of sub-rules **because m charges every output with multiplicity**. Translating it into a bound on a deduplicated container union requires bounded overlap, a family-size allowance, or the same charging convention. |
| L1 | **FIX** | Invoke Theorem N with its actual hypotheses: \(16Y\le P^\theta,\theta<1/2\), invertible weights, and \(|V||S|=o(P)\). These hold at the attaining scales, but do not follow from Theorem L’s general modulus condition. The conclusion concerns rules valid for all locally uncovered pairs. |
| L1: Theorem-G charging and “no better than brute-force” | **FIX** | Require nonzero values of absolute size below N, applicable polynomial/box hypotheses, and separate-call budget \(\sum_b\sqrt{V_b}\). Theorem G supplies an algorithmic upper bound, not a universal runtime lower bound. The resulting obstruction is within this accounting model, up to logarithms. |
| L2 | **WRONG** | The \(55/62\) threshold is correct for a common observation scale; use the saturated bound \(\min(P,\Delta/M)\). The minimum over sub-rules cannot force **each** sub-rule to observe 69/124 of the bits: append an empty, uninformative sub-rule. Nor does observing that many bits imply use of the exact integer hyperbola. The threshold is “at least,” not strictly “more than.” |
| L3 | **CORRECT** | The arithmetic checks: \((2M/\Delta)(2\Delta/(PM)+1)\le8/P\), hence at most \(4P\max m=O(XP^{3/2})\) caught pairs and density \(n^{O(1)}N^{-7/124}\). Retain alignment and summed-mass assumptions. “Equivalent” means contraposition, not an efficient reduction between algorithms. |
| Prop D | **FIX** | The union-bound inequality is correct. Show that the endpoint error is negligible when deriving \(\sum\rho_\ell\ge1-o(1)\); adjust integer rounding for container capacities. Then \(|S|\gtrsim Y/|V|\asymp J/\lambda=J^{4/5}=N^{4/31+o(1)}\) follows. This bounds **exhaustive covering of locally admissible integers**, not every method locating p. The total-variation statement is correct within the independent-coordinate model. |
| Cor. D2 | **FIX** | The Minkowski example is valid; I checked it through \(\ell=400\). Its proof is separate from S’s \(\ell>2H^2\) regime. Prop D forces a sum of densities, not the stated lower bound at every prime. Charging \(sH^2\asymp sY\), DCL extraction and CRT decoding gives \(PY^{-3/2}+sY\), minimized at \(Y\asymp P^{2/5}\) for bounded s: \(N^{1/5}\). This recovers that exponent in this design family; it does not classify all bounded-prime inverse theorems. |
| “At most three primes per Coppersmith modulus” | **WRONG** | Correct only under the extra restriction \(M\le P\). Coppersmith does not impose that upper bound: a known factor residue modulo a larger modulus is easier to decode. The blind round explicitly identified this distinction. |
| Prop Q | **CORRECT** | The adaptive equality-query bound and \(N^{-1/20+o(1)}\) exponent are correct. Its arithmetic transfer remains unproved, as disclosed. |
| Prop I: Green–Harper | **FIX** | The listed hypotheses are substantially correct. Say noncoverage supplies insufficient restrictions to invoke them informatively. Allowed classes being nearly full does **not** prove that actual U occupies nearly every class. [Source](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1311.6176). |
| Prop I: Helfgott–Venkatesh | **FIX** | The two-dimensional hypothesis is correct. \(U\times\{0\}\) satisfies it with an already known line; exact divisor pairs supply an already known hyperbola. “No informative new conclusion” is more accurate than “does not apply.” [Source](https://webusers.imj-prg.fr/~harald.helfgott/texts/howsmall.pdf). |
| Prop I: Walsh | **FIX** | Separate the Duke 2012 high-dimensional theorem, [1105.1551](https://arxiv.org/html/1105.1551v2), from the later algebraicity theorem, [1307.0259](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1307.0259). Their hypotheses do not follow from the band exclusions; singleton conclusions disclose no unknown coefficient efficiently. |
| Prop I: Gallagher/Croot–Elsholtz | **CORRECT** | Substituting the allowed-complement cardinalities gives a negative larger-sieve denominator or a trivial improvement. Include Croot–Elsholtz’s cardinality premise; these are cardinality bounds rather than constructive classifications. [Source](https://ecroot.math.gatech.edu/gallagher.pdf). |
| Prop I: Croot–Mao–Pohoata–Yip | **FIX** | Proposition 5.1 is accurately identified. The \(e^{o(1)}\) gain is what the **supplied complement bound alone certifies**; it is not an upper bound on the gain using actual occupancies. At most one attaining-scale band prime fits its product restriction. No suitable container-generation algorithm follows. [Source](https://arxiv.org/html/2607.15311v2#S5). |
| §7 E1: P1, P2, P3, P5, P5b, P6, P8c | **CORRECT** | Their reported frozen passes and tabulated ratios reproduce. Describe them as passes of specified tolerances, not proofs of pseudorandomness. |
| E1: P4 | **FIX** | The three failures are correctly reported and point away from concentration. The proposed “proved mechanism” is incomplete: individually balanced AP pieces do not prove a variance deficit for their overlapping union. Label the explanation unverified. |
| E1: P4b | **FIX** | The frozen failure and ratio 0.78078 are correct. Actual totals are **26 observed versus 33.3 expected**, not 34 versus 44. Under a simple Poisson approximation this is about −1.27σ; that approximation itself is not established here. |
| E1: missing classes and trajectories | **WRONG** | “No missing classes anywhere” ignores the eligibility filter. Exact counterexample: 24S, idx 1, \(N=11954381\), \(|U|=102\), misses class 6 mod 23; mod 23 was ineligible. Say none occurred among eligible tests. Maximum pooled trajectory deviation is **1.702%**, not 1.3%. |
| E2: P7 | **CORRECT** | Medians, ranges and all 250 multiplicative identities reproduce. The P7 Boolean omits the registered identity requirement, although the separate identity check passes on these data; repair the Boolean. |
| E3: P8 | **FIX** | The frozen pooled pass and reported numbers reproduce. Confidence intervals and KS p-values treat observations as independent despite shared p values; provide inference grouped by p, or label these nominal statistics. A pooled pass does not establish absence of conditional structure. |
| Matched controls and fresh-seed replication | **FIX** | Their exploratory status and changed controls are honestly disclosed; reported numbers reproduce. Fresh replication also gives CF-distribution KS \(p=0.309\), supporting non-replication of that signal. L* was not saved in the replication output, so do not imply that its original distributional signal was independently replicated. Replace “so no structure” with “these follow-ups supplied no reproducible useful enrichment.” |
| Held-out deficit follow-up | **FIX** | Re-running the scripts confirms the deficit and approximate full-band ratios 0.87/0.80/0.76. Exploratory and mechanism-unverified labels are appropriate. “Absent at the real scale” is too strong: \(Y^2<P\) removes the general size-based exclusion, not every occurrence of small \(\delta_v\). |
| E4: singleton observation and selection bias | **CORRECT** | Independently confirmed \(U=\{p\}\) in all 88 records. The 156-fold unconditioned comparison is selection-biased. Zero additional survivors versus 0.565 predicted, and 40,389 versus 40,365.8 for smaller J, are consistent with the model; they do not validate the full selection-conditioned law. |
| E4: P9 | **FIX** | L* passes, but the NN endpoint is **not evaluable**, rather than passed. Code treating `None` as success must be disclosed as a partial endpoint evaluation. There are also only 83 distinct pairs among the 88 records, affecting nominal KS calibration. |
| E4: P9b | **CORRECT** | The censored excluded-\((1,1)\) artefact is supported. Restrict the statement to censored records; several uncensored signed J=2 records do not have \(p\equiv q\bmod\ell_1\). |
| E4: locating the singleton “needs” sieving or combining many primes | **WRONG** | Neither Prop D nor Theorem L proves this computational dichotomy. A singleton’s existence supplies no efficient locator, but that does not establish the claimed exhaustive list of possible algorithms. |
| §7 final decision | **FIX** | Report four failed configuration-level predictions, an unevaluable P9 endpoint, and exploratory follow-ups. “No demonstrated useful concentration in the tested statistics” is justified. A general kill, universal pseudorandomness, or literal satisfaction of the all-pass frozen condition is not. |

**Overall verdict: REJECT as written.** The restricted counting barriers and much of the experimental work are sound. The central general refutation depends on unsupported computational lower bounds and stronger conclusions than either the proofs or experiments establish.